WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
Staged rollouts: shadow, canary, ramp.

2
00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:05.400
Don’t move from zero users to all users.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.179
Common stages for an LLM feature: Internal use.

4
00:00:09.459 --> 00:00:11.613
Your own team uses it on real work.

5
00:00:11.893 --> 00:00:14.560
You’ll find embarrassing failures quickly and cheaply.

6
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.885
Shadow mode.

7
00:00:16.165 --> 00:00:21.584
The new version runs on real traffic, but users still see the old behavior (or nothing).

8
00:00:21.864 --> 00:00:24.296
You compare outputs offline with your graders.

9
00:00:24.576 --> 00:00:33.536
This is the safest way to test a new prompt or model on production inputs, at the cost of paying for two calls per request during the shadow period.

10
00:00:33.816 --> 00:00:34.627
Canary.

11
00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:37.616
A small share of real users get the new version.

12
00:00:37.896 --> 00:00:51.613
Google’s SRE workbook describes how to do this well: the canary must be large enough and run long enough to be representative, including peak load; compare canary against a control group running the old version at the same time,

13
00:00:51.893 --> 00:01:09.515
rather than comparing before and after (time of day and day of week confound that); break metrics down by population, because a bad canary hides in whole-service averages; prefer metrics tied to what users experience; and run one canary at a time so the signals don’t mix.

14
00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:10.499
Ramp.

15
00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:18.096
Increase the share in steps, with written success criteria at each step and a named person who decides whether to proceed.

16
00:01:18.376 --> 00:01:23.837
If canary metrics diverge from control, pause and roll back first, then investigate.

17
00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:38.560
For LLM features, add semantic metrics to the canary comparison: online eval pass rate on sampled traffic, refusal rate, guard block rate, escalation-to-human rate, thumbs-down rate, and cost per conversation.

18
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.701
Several of these move when quality changes even though nothing errors.
